1. INTERVIEW READY ANSWERS
======================================================================

Q1. What is an Array?

Answer:
An array is a linear data structure that stores multiple elements of the same data type in contiguous memory locations. It provides O(1) random access using indexing.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q2. Why do we need an Array?

Answer:
Before arrays, we had to create multiple variables to store similar data. Arrays allow us to store multiple values of the same type under a single variable name, making data management easier.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q3. Why is array access O(1)?

Answer:
Array elements are stored in contiguous memory locations. Using the base address and index, the address of any element can be calculated directly.

Address(arr[i])
=
Base Address + (i × Size of Element)

Therefore, no traversal is required.

Time Complexity = O(1)

------------------------------------------------------------

Q4. Why is searching in an array O(n)?

Answer:
During searching, the index of the required element is unknown. Therefore, we may need to traverse every element until the target is found.

Worst Case = O(n)

------------------------------------------------------------

Q5. Advantages of Arrays

Answer:
• Stores multiple values under one variable.
• Stores homogeneous data.
• O(1) random access.
• Easy traversal using loops.
• Cache friendly due to contiguous memory.
• Less memory overhead than linked lists.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q6. Disadvantages of Arrays

Answer:
• Fixed size after creation.
• Costly insertion and deletion.
• Memory wastage if size is overallocated.
• Cannot directly store multiple primitive data types.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q7. Why are insertion and deletion expensive?

Answer:
Arrays use contiguous memory. Inserting or deleting at the beginning or middle requires shifting elements.

Insertion

Beginning
→ O(n)

Middle
→ O(n)

End
→ O(1) (if free space exists)

Deletion

Beginning
→ O(n)

Middle
→ O(n)

End
→ O(1)

------------------------------------------------------------

Q8. Can arrays store different data types?

Answer:
Normally, an array stores elements of a single declared type.

int[]
→ Only int values

String[]
→ Only String objects

Object[]
→ Any Java object (String, Integer, Double, User-defined class, etc.)

------------------------------------------------------------

Q9. What is ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException?

Answer:
It is a Runtime (Unchecked) Exception that occurs when an invalid index is accessed.

Valid Index

0 to length - 1

Prevention

• Check index before access.
• Use arr.length while traversing.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q10. Why are arrays stored in contiguous memory?

Answer:
Contiguous memory allows direct address calculation using the index, resulting in O(1) random access. It also improves CPU cache performance.

